WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.795
- Please welcome, Michelle Buteau.

00:00:02.795 --> 00:00:06.452
(audience applauding)

00:00:06.452 --> 00:00:07.619
- Out of time!

00:00:08.623 --> 00:00:12.780
- I had the privilege of working with you

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.410
and it was the last film I've done

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.873
and probably will do for a little while.

00:00:19.873 --> 00:00:21.160
- I was so intimidated
being on set with you

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:24.340
and it was also my first
jump back into work

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:25.350
when the babies were born.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.410
The babies were six weeks old

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.650
and at that point it was
just like, every two hours.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:33.220
It was just like crazy,
we need bottles, diapers.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.950
But, I just remember thinking at one point

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.690
you brought your daughter to set

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:40.620
and it was an overnight shoot

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.800
and it wasn't too late yet

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.771
and I was like, this is what it is, right?

00:00:43.771 --> 00:00:47.370
This is how you sort of, do it all.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.075
You sort of try to make
time for everybody.

00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:51.960
And it looked like you guys were so close,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.360
like you almost had your own language.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.810
And I'm like, this is so beautiful.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.870
And I feel like, also to
see you in your element,

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.840
you know, motherhood and also acting.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.290
That's the best education
anybody could ever have,

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:04.123
you know what I mean?

00:01:04.123 --> 00:01:05.270
So, thank you for that.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.430
It was really fun.

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.440
I mean, I hope we get to work again.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.860
- I would give anything
to collaborate with you.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.873
It's so funny how we all
scramble so much as women.

00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:17.030
And here's what's so funny,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.770
is I don't feel like men do that scramble.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.020
Why don't men flip out
about all the details?

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.890
And why do we?

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.570
- I do think it's a biological thing.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:28.630
And I hate to say that,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.320
because I don't want this to turn into

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.370
an episode of "Everybody Loves Raymond",

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.550
but I think it just is.

00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.300
When my kid cries, I know
what kind of cry that is.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.737
I'm like, "They're hungry,
they're needy, they're bored,

00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:40.700
"they've hurt themselves."

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.060
I even know the cry and I'm not relaxed

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.100
until I help them relax.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.350
Watching you, I was like,
okay this is what it is.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.270
You can have a handle
on it, you can leave,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.100
you can bring them,
you can make it happen.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.790
It really is, I think,
creating their own world

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.230
and level of comfort for what you can do

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.730
and not being caught in
everyone else's rat race.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.060
Because, quite frankly,
that's just going to age me.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:09.860
- I love how when your man, who is Dutch

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.860
and you guys have furniture
store in Van Der Most Modern

00:02:14.994 --> 00:02:17.360
in Brooklyn.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:18.520
- Yes!

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.220
It's really fun to go help somebody

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:24.030
make their house what they
thought they could make it.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.080
If that makes sense.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:26.820
- How did you meet your man?

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.390
- Oh girl!

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.749
Well my mom's watching, bleh!

00:02:30.749 --> 00:02:34.130
(laughing) I was out one night,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.440
and you know, dippin'
and doin' it, dancin',

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.590
he was spinning me,
buying expensive drinks.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.890
Very overprices margaritas,
yes, using real lime.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.140
And we just sort of kept
in touch (chuckles).

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:49.040
- Now were you a fan of reality TV

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.670
because obviously "The
Circle" is a big phenomenon

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.455
and you're actually
getting on a plane tomorrow

00:02:54.455 --> 00:02:58.520
to go back to Manchester
to film season two.

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:01.170
- Yes, I think it's not only a fun watch

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.700
but it's also really important.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.460
What happens when you have
to engage in social media?

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.710
You also realize the back
stories behind catfishing

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.630
where people don't feel worthy

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:14.060
to show who they really
are on social media.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.850
Which is really sad too, and
that begs the question, why?

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.790
You know, we are always enough!

00:03:19.790 --> 00:03:21.630
Like be kind to each other.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.730
You can say all these comments, right?

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.700
But, just think about it

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.560
because this is someone's
son or someone's daughter.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.060
So, I really love being
a part of "The Circle"

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.680
and I cannot wait to see
what kind of characters

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.490
they've casted for season two.

00:03:37.490 --> 00:03:38.650
- Oh, and your book, by the way,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.807
comes out in December, oh my God!

00:03:40.807 --> 00:03:42.040
"Survival of the Thickest"!

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.090
- Yeah! I wrote a book!

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.020
What is wrong with her, she wrote a book?

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.950
It's called "Survival of the Thickest".

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.588
It's plus-sized essays
in a small-minded world.

00:03:50.588 --> 00:03:52.660
And it's available for pre-order

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.190
and comes out December 8th,

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.710
just in time for Hanukkah,
Christmas, Kwanza

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:58.690
and non-denominational whatever.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:02.510
It's stories that don't
necessarily fit on stage.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:05.540
But for better or for worse,

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:09.630
I've found that there's real
power in sharing your truth.

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.080
So this was a moment, this was a moment.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.520
And I hope people read it and enjoy it.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.230
Or at least talk about it over Zoom.

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:20.800
- Well I now know what
I am doing for Christmas

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:24.120
'cause before I had my
kids I was a real weirdo.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.370
I didn't really hang out
with anyone on Christmas.

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:29.220
It was time for me to kind
of catch up on solo time.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.700
But I would always read a book.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.300
And it would be like,
"Letters to a Young Poet"

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.280
by Rainer Maria Rilke,
like really heavy stuff.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:38.067
And then one Christmas I discovered

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.633
"Holidays On Ice" by David Sedaris.

00:04:42.224 --> 00:04:43.074
- My favorite.

00:04:43.074 --> 00:04:45.270
- And I was like, why am I
reading such heavy stuff?

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.200
Why am I being so bah humbug on Christmas?

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.750
I need to lighten up, so I am so excited.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.300
This is going to be the
best Christmas read ever.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.790
I'm going to pre-order
today, I'm so excited.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:04:59.260
Thank you
- Oh my goodness.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:00.960
- so much Michelle.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.745
- Thank you! I love you so much, girl!

00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:05.147
- All right, Michelle Buteau.

00:05:05.147 --> 00:05:07.680
"Welcome to Buteaupia" is on Netflix now.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.810
Pre-order Michelle's book,
"Survival of the Thickest"

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.720
and obviously "The Circle"
is available on Netflix

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.970
and Michelle is leaving
to go film season two,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:17.273
right after this.

00:05:17.273 --> 00:05:19.856
(upbeat music)

